                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 490</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>The Presidential candidates for the elections in May were announced by the
                    various political parties in late February and early March. The nominee for the
                    People’s Party was Heinrich Gleissner,
                    Governor of Upper Austria. His selection caused some concern in the Legation
                    because of his former affiliation with the Nazi Party, a fact which Donnelly felt could be exploited by the
                    Communists. (Telegram 1373 from Vienna, January 8, 763.11/1–851) The Socialist
                    nominee was Theodor Koerner, Mayor of Vienna, while the recently formed
                    Nonpartisan Committee for National Unity selected Burghard Breitner, a hero of
                    World War I and a professor of surgery at the University of Innsbruck.</p>
                <p>Since no candidate polled an absolute majority during the elections of May 6, a
                    runoff election was scheduled for May 27 between Gleissner and Koerner, the two leading candidates. Despite all
                    preelection analyses, the Socialist candidate Koerner was elected Rehner’s
                    successor by 52 percent of the total vote. Documentation concerning the election
                    is in files 763.00 and 763.11.</p>
            </div>
